WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.837
Reading the MTEB leaderboard without being misled.

2
00:00:03.117 --> 00:00:14.509
The Massive Text Embedding Benchmark (MTEB; Muennighoff and colleagues, 2022) was built because embedding models were often evaluated on a narrow set of tasks.

3
00:00:14.789 --> 00:00:26.928
The original release covered 8 task types (retrieval, reranking, clustering, classification, semantic similarity, and others) over 58 datasets and 112 languages.

4
00:00:27.208 --> 00:00:34.035
Its headline finding is still the most useful one: no single embedding method dominated across all tasks.

5
00:00:34.315 --> 00:00:56.608
MMTEB (Enevoldsen and colleagues, 2025) expanded it to over 500 tasks in more than 250 languages, and found that the best publicly available model on its multilingual benchmark was multilingual-e5-large-instruct, at 560 million parameters, ahead of far larger LLM-based models.

6
00:00:56.888 --> 00:01:00.451
The public leaderboard is the best starting point for a shortlist.

7
00:01:00.731 --> 00:01:02.651
Treat it as that, for four reasons.

8
00:01:02.931 --> 00:01:04.531
Averages hide your task.

9
00:01:04.811 --> 00:01:07.029
The overall score blends task types.

10
00:01:07.309 --> 00:01:10.787
For search, look at the retrieval columns, for your languages.

11
00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:12.219
Domain mismatch.

12
00:01:12.499 --> 00:01:16.637
Benchmarks are mostly public web, Wikipedia, and academic text.

13
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:23.403
BEIR (Thakur and colleagues, 2021) showed how much retrieval quality can drop out of domain.

14
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:28.333
Your support tickets, contracts, or clinical policies are a different distribution.

15
00:01:28.613 --> 00:01:30.512
Training on the test distribution.

16
00:01:30.792 --> 00:01:46.685
The MTEB maintainers (Chung and colleagues, 2025) report that the highest-ranking models on the legacy English leaderboard achieved their scores partly by training on benchmark tasks, while some lower-ranked models may generalize better.

17
00:01:46.965 --> 00:01:55.712
They now ask model authors to disclose training data and compute a “zero-shot score” from how many benchmark datasets a model trained on.

18
00:01:55.992 --> 00:01:59.064
Check it, and know it only captures disclosed overlap.

19
00:01:59.344 --> 00:02:01.563
Size and latency aren’t in the score.

20
00:02:01.843 --> 00:02:09.843
A model a few points higher that is twenty times larger may be the wrong trade for a latency budget or a CPU-only deployment.
